backend composition main  main lifecycle hook apps/main every entity has exactly one schema definition across slices and apps. apis come in two kinds: client (user-facing) and m2m (service-to-service). five layers, one responsibility each: middleware · router · service · repository · model. the substrate is shared and transport-agnostic; only the edge changes. service, repository and model never know how they were reached. each kind of caller — client, m2m, background — brings its own router and middlewares, and calls the same substrate. the call graph is fixed. a client — the api consumer — calls only the router. the router calls its middlewares, then one service. a middleware calls a repository for hydration reads only. a service calls its own entity's repository, never another entity's. cross-entity calls are service to service; across backends, over the m2m edge. only a repository calls the database, through the model. a repository carries no application logic; a model carries no logic. background works (cron jobs, agents, pipelines) plug into the service layer or run independently. many callers reach the same slice operations — client requests, m2m calls, crons, agents. layering puts the logic below the transport, in one substrate every caller passes through: the edge adapts the caller, the substrate holds the logic. adding a caller or a datastore means writing an edge, not reimplementing the core. and because every slice's api has the same five layers and the same strict chain, a developer knows where anything lives before opening a folder. entry points and request classes the substrate — service, repository, model, ac engine — is reached from different edges. what differs per caller is middleware and router; the substrate is shared. mod12 client-facing and machine-to-machine routes are distinct top-level paths: `/api/client/*` for end-user agents, `/api/m2m/*` for server-to-server. an external provider that ships its own router mounts under those two namespaces — `api/client/<provider>/*` — never alongside them, so `client/*` covers every endpoint a client hits. 03 draft auth endpoints are their own request class: `/api/client/auth/*` is provider-owned, session lifecycle only, and access-context hydration never runs on it. 03 draft api3 backends with no api surface (e.g. cron over self-contained libraries) are exempt from the 5-layer/2-path rule. scf10 process singletons — the server, the db pool, the redis connection — are created once, in top-level files at the app's `src/` root: one file, one instantiation. edges differ; the substrate is shared ascii http client     ─→ middleware (session)  ─┐
http m2m        ─→ middleware (service)  ─┤      build
in-process cron ─────────────────────────►├─→   access  ─→ service ─→ engine ─→ repository ─→ db
agent runtime   ─────────────────────────►│    context             (filters)
autonomous worker ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────→ repository ─→ db  the five layers api1 five layers, one responsibility each: middleware (hydration/auth) → router (http transport) → service (business logic) → repository (data access) → model. api2 only repositories touch the database — read and write, no exception. two valid paths: middleware→router→service→repo→db, or middleware→repo→db (hydration reads only). scf2 one abstraction per file, maximally separated. what each layer owns — and what it must never do html ../assets/backend-composition/five-layers.html the data path — any other layer calling the db is a violation ascii   requester     router       middleware       service     repository       db
  ─────────     ──────       ──────────       ───────     ──────────       ──
      │           │              │               │            │             │
