findallbyorgids(/* … */): promise<{ rows: orgactivityrow[]; total: number }>; models, schemas and data shape one hand-written shape per entity — the model. everything below it is inferred or derived, so the compiler chains every schema and type back to the model and nothing drifts silently. scf4 one canonical **model** per entity, one declarer — re-declaring elsewhere is a violation. models carry no business logic. scf3 models: `<entity>.model.ts` + `<entity>.schemas.ts` side by side, in a folder named `models` (never `schemas`). scf5 the model is the only hand-written entity shape. entity zod schemas are inferred from it (`createselectschema` / `createinsertschema`); types exist only through `z.infer`. a hand-written entity schema or a hand-written interface duplicating one is a violation. an input schema is a derivation: `entityschema.pick/omit/extend` plus refinements. the diff is the only hand-authored part — it is the business decision of what a caller may set. a from-scratch `z.object` over entity fields is a violation. a composed shape — a wire view, an app-specific type — takes its entity fields from the inferred schemas (`schema.shape.field`) and declares only what is genuinely new (computed fields). the service composing it returns `satisfies` the composed type, so drifting from the model is a compile error. wire schemas are the client contract and live in the slice's `shared/`; web infers its types from them — never from entity schemas, so the persistence shape never leaks to a client. scf7 module entities in `<seed slice>/api/models/`; product slice entities in `apps/main/slices/product/<f>/api/models/` — same shape, one declarer each; `src/models/index.ts` re-exports only (db-client aggregation), declares nothing. api34 immutable classification fields (e.g. org type) never change post-creation; denormalize them onto dependent records for convenience. api35 request-scoped, client-owned state (e.g. active org) is never persisted — the client sends it every request; the server re-derives and re-checks. (exception: real-time server-initiated actions.)  the chain, end to end one rename in the model — `name` → `displayname` — breaks the build at every derivation and every `satisfies`, pointing at exactly what must follow. written once — the model // organization.model.ts
export const uomorganization = pgtable("organization", {
  id: text("id").primarykey(),
  name: text("name").notnull(),
  logo: text("logo"),
  createdat: timestamp("created_at").notnull(),
}); inferred, never written // organization.schemas.ts
export const organizationschema = createselectschema(uomorganization);
