interface orgcard { id: string; title: string } naming api24 repos use `find*`; services use `get*`; `load*` is reserved for config — and a config loader is never named like a db accessor. layers don't share one name: repo `findbyx` → service `getbyx`. api25 `getmy*` is allowed in services. api26 multi-record lookups are plural and take array params; singular names only for single-unique-entity lookups. api27 mutate endpoints are named `update`, not `patch`. api28 recovery/reset endpoints are named after the slice object (`completepasswordrecovery`), not generic verbs. api29 public endpoint names never leak internal mechanism (`register`, not `flows`); services are named for what they do, not how (`auth`, not `relay`). api30 `getcurrent*` is `getlatest*` when no rollback exists — names reflect what the system actually does. api31 `me` namespace = data of the requesting user and no organization. a route is `me` only if both hold: it touches no other user's data, and it works without an active org. fails the first → `admin` or the entity's route; fails the second → the entity's route under the active org. `me` routes are exactly the ones allowed to run with no active-org context. api32 route grouping follows the primary entity retrieved, not url nesting (`/organization/invitations` is an invitation route); a route path need not match its service name. api33 identity-provider-specific lookups never live in the generic user model/service — they belong to the integration layer.   hooks and webhooks mod4 a [[lifecycle hook|hook]] carries the bare fact only — message/template content is exported separately. mod5 a seed slice that receives an external webhook owns it exclusively and translates it into an internal lifecycle hook; other seed slices react to the hook, never the webhook.   external providers an external provider is a system a seed slice collaborates with that owns its own persistence. providers do not appear in the five-layer model: their internal writes are theirs, and the seed slice's responsibility ends at the adapter call. prv1 every touch-point with an external provider routes through one abstraction/integration layer in its owning seed slice. app code never calls provider endpoints or imports the provider directly — the app must not even know the provider's name. providers are called from the **service** layer, optionally through a seed slice-owned **adapter** — a thin facade that translates between our slice and the provider's api. adapters carry no business logic. 03 draft a provider's internal storage is not our database, even when co-deployed — provider calls do not violate "only repositories access our database". mirroring provider state into our db goes through a repository like any other write; the adapter or service sequences the provider call and the mirror write. 03 draft scf13 integration code for a **consumed** provider lives in its own folder, not `api/` — `api/` is what you expose, not what you consume.  the identity provider, in the repo today uom consumes kratos. the seed slice declares a provider-agnostic port; everything kratos-specific — client, adapter, infra — lives in its own `identity/` folder. nothing outside the seed slice knows the provider's name, and the rest of uom depends on the port, not on kratos. apps/main/slices/seed/user-organization-mgmt/api/identity/ identity/
├── identity-provider.port.ts        the provider-agnostic contract
└── kratos/
    ├── kratos-client.ts             http calls to the provider
    ├── kratos-identity-provider.ts  implements the port
    └── infra/                       the provider's own runtime (docker-compose) list endpoints every endpoint returning a collection follows one standard contract via the `list-query` lib — no carve-outs. query params: `page` · `pagesize` · `sort` (whitelisted per endpoint, mandatory default stable sort, unknown field → 400) · `search` (only on endpoints declaring themselves searchable, unsupported → 400, never silent-ignore). response: `{ rows, total }`. 03 draft always paginated, defaults always apply. `total` reflects the full scoped count — same where as the list query, never diverging. past-end returns `{ rows: [], total }`, never 404. a stable tiebreaker column is appended as the final sort field — pagination without one is forbidden. 03 draft   unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed

doc/gated-scoped-ac-ac-patterns:
ac patterns gated-scoped-ac  gated-scoped-ac consumer shared/libs/gated-scoped-ac   axioms every storage operation is authorized through the engine. authorization narrows reachable rows and accessible fields. reads narrow at the repository; writes gate at the service. a write against a specific row passes that row's identity through the engine's filter — by submission (create) or by repository confirmation (update, delete). every authorization decision is observable. purpose **why.** without a fixed pattern per operation, every developer rediscovers when to call which `resolve*` method, where to gate, and what to log. drift produces silent over- or under-permission. **how.** reads narrow the result set inside the repository; writes gate before executing inside the service. each operation has between one and two gates depending on whether the row's full identity is known up-front. **why this way.** reads need narrowing, not a yes/no — narrowing is the result. writes need a yes/no, not narrowing — and that yes/no must include any field of the target row the rules reference. the two-gate pattern for update/delete is the only way to answer "can this user touch *this* row" when the rule conditions on fields the caller didn't submit. **what.** the patterns a service or repository uses to call the engine for each crud operation. one pattern per (operation × execution target). reference › crud permutation matrix  ascii | operation | execution target | gates | where they live | engine call |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **create** | repository (internal) | 1 | service | `engine.resolvecreate(roles, entity, subjects, properties)` |
| **create** | external provider | 1 | service | `engine.resolvecreate(...)` |
| **read** | repository (internal) | 1 | repository | `engine.resolveread(roles, entity, subjects)` |
| **update** | repository (internal) | 2 | service (gate 1+2 collapsed) | `repo.findbyidforallow(id, accesscontext)` → boolean |
| **update** | external provider | 2 | service (gate 1+2 collapsed) | `repo.findbyidforallow(id, accesscontext)` → boolean |
| **delete** (id only) | repository / external | 2 | service | `repo.findbyidforallow(id, accesscontext)` → boolean |
| **delete** (row in hand) | repository / external | 1 | service | `engine.resolvedelete(roles, entity, subjects, rowproperties)` | there is no `read / external provider` row: external providers are write-only in this architecture; reads always end at the repository. reference › pattern: create `candidateproperties` come from the validated request body. untrusted ids inside those properties remain a vulnerability surface — same as any input, validate before passing. **why one gate.** the candidate row's properties are what the caller is about to write. authorization is a pure function of `(effectiveroles, subjects, properties)` — no db lookup needed. // service
const verdict = engine.resolvecreate(
  accesscontext.effectiveroles,
  "<entity>",
  accesscontext.subjects,
  candidateproperties,
);
audit.info({ entity, action: "create", decision: verdict.allow ? "allowed" : "denied", effectiveroles, properties }, "access decision");
if (!verdict.allow) throw forbidden();

// internal:        await repo.create(candidateproperties);
// external:        await externalprovider.create(candidateproperties); ts reference › pattern: read `where` may be `undefined` (allow all rows). when no role grants read, `where` evaluates to `1 = 0` and `select` is empty — the query returns zero rows naturally without special-casing. the service layer for reads is a thin pass-through. no service-side switch is needed. **why one gate, and why at the repo.** the engine's `where` / `joins` / `select` is the result narrowing — it must be applied at the sql boundary, not at the service. // repository
const { where, joins, select } = engine.resolveread(
  accesscontext.effectiveroles,
  "<entity>",
  accesscontext.subjects,
);

let q = db.select(select).from(<table>);
for (const j of joins) q = q.leftjoin(j.table, j.on);
return q.where(and(<other-conditions>, where)); ts reference › pattern: update `findbyidforallow` is the canonical name and the canonical contract: "does the engine's update filter, applied to the user's effective roles, include this id?" it returns `boolean`. it does **not** mutate. it does **not** return the row contents. returning `boolean` keeps gate 2 unable to leak data when gate 1 has already approved the role. gate 1 (service)     role check: is any rule potentially applicable?
   ↓ if allow
findbyidforallow      repo lookup: is this row inside the engine's where for update?
   ↓ if returns true
gate 2 (service)      execute the update via repo or external provider // service
const allowed = await repo.findbyidforallow(payload.id, accesscontext);
audit.info({ entity, action: "update", decision: allowed ? "allowed" : "denied", targetids: [payload.id], effectiveroles }, "access decision");
if (!allowed) throw forbidden();

// internal:        return repo.update(payload.id, payload.data);
// external:        return externalprovider.update(payload.id, payload.data); ts // repository
async findbyidforallow(id, accesscontext): promise<boolean> {
  const { where, joins } = engine.resolveupdate(
    accesscontext.effectiveroles,
    "<entity>",
    accesscontext.subjects,
  );
  let q = db.select({ id: <table>.id }).from(<table>);
  for (const j of joins) q = q.leftjoin(j.table, j.on);
  const rows = await q.where(and(eq(<table>.id, id), where));
  return rows.length > 0;
} ts **why two gates.** update rules typically reference fields the caller does not have at hand — e.g. `entity.workspaceid:$session.activeworkspaceid` requires reading the row's `workspaceid` from storage. the decision cannot come from the request body alone. reference › pattern: delete **variant a — caller has only the id.** identical to update. **variant b — caller has the row already (or a reliable identifier subset).** if the row is already loaded for unrelated reasons, pass its properties to `resolvedelete` directly. **when to choose each:** variant a is the default. variant b is an optimization when the row is already in hand. **do not** fetch the row solely to use variant b — if you must call the db anyway, use variant a and keep gate 2 explicit. two flavors, chosen by what the caller already has. // service
const allowed = await repo.findbyidforallow(id, accesscontext);
audit.info({ entity, action: "delete", decision: allowed ? "allowed" : "denied", targetids: [id], effectiveroles }, "access decision");
if (!allowed) throw forbidden();

// internal:    return repo.delete(id);
// external:    return externalprovider.delete(id); ts const target = await repo.findbyuserandorg(...);  // already fetched for business reasons
if (!target) throw notfound();

const verdict = engine.resolvedelete(
  accesscontext.effectiveroles,
  "<entity>",
  accesscontext.subjects,
  { organizationid: target.organizationid, ...other identifying fields },
);
if (!verdict.allow) throw forbidden();

// internal:    return repo.delete(target.id);
// external:    return externalprovider.delete(target.id); ts reference › audit logging logs go to the **audit channel** (typically `logger.child({ channel: "audit" })`), not the diagnostic logger. audit logs may be retained for compliance; diagnostic logs may not. reads typically do not log per-row; they may log aggregate access events at the route boundary. every gate that produces a decision must be logged before the throw: ascii | field | required | purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `userid` | yes | who asked |
| `activeorgid` (or equivalent tenant key) | yes | where they asked |
| `entity` | yes | what kind of thing |
| `action` | yes | `read` / `create` / `update` / `delete` |
| `decision` | yes | `"allowed"` or `"denied"` |
| `effectiveroles` | yes | what roles produced the decision |
| `targetids` | for update / delete | the row id |
| `properties` | for create only | the candidate row's properties (validate before logging — never log secrets) | reference › field-level write gating — known gap  today the engine evaluates target scopes against `properties` for writes but does not narrow the *writable column set* against the role's `fields` allowlist. until that is implemented, treat field-level write authorization as a **todo at the service layer** — between the verdict and the executor — using the same `fields` array the role declares. document such checks inline so they migrate cleanly when the engine surfaces this natively. reference › forbidden patterns **repository re-resolves on writes.** write repos must not call `engine.resolvecreate/update/delete` themselves. the service makes the verdict; the repo only executes. (read repos call `engine.resolveread` because narrowing is the result.) **service skips `findbyidforallow` and trusts the id from the request body for update/delete.** gate 2 is mandatory unless variant b applies. **service trusts client-supplied properties for create.** validate properties via the same mechanism as any other request input before passing to `resolvecreate`. **repository returns the row from `findbyidforallow`.** it returns `boolean` only. the next step's executor fetches the row by id if needed. **skipping the audit log on a deny.** a deny is the security-relevant event; not logging it loses the trail. **hand-rolling a role check anywhere in the codebase.** authorization is the engine's job — re-implementing it bypasses the rules and is the most common source of permission bugs. unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed

doc/gated-scoped-ac-bag:
scope context gated-scoped-ac  gated-scoped-ac consumer shared/libs/gated-scoped-ac   axioms scopecontext is a bag of subject rows plus the caller's assigned roles — the entire authority surface at evaluation time. the bag's shape is declared by the consumer in `subjects.config.ts`. the library has no built-in shape. the bag is built per request from trusted sources by the consumer. the library does not build it. purpose **why.** authorization depends on the caller's state at the moment of the request, not at config time. rules reference subject paths (`$session.user.id`, `$session.team.id`); the bag is what those paths resolve against. **how.** the consumer declares the subject names their rules will reference (`subjects.config.ts`). at request time, the consumer populates the bag from trusted sources — db rows, verified session, whatever is authoritative for that subject — and hands it to the engine. **why this way.** the library cannot know what "session" or "team" means in a given product. by making the bag's shape a parameter and leaving population to the consumer, the library stays product-agnostic and the consumer keeps full control over the trust boundary. **what.** the runtime input to the engine — a bag carrying the caller's subject data (whatever identifies "who is asking, in what setting") and assigned roles, passed in per call. reference  examples use the same fictional document-management product as dsl-rules (`dsl-rules.md`): workspaces contain folders, folders contain documents, users may belong to teams. none of this vocabulary is built into the library. reference › the two halves **subjects** — a bag of named rows describing the caller's identity and state. **assigned roles** — the scoped role names the caller holds. both are needed for every evaluation: subjects feed rule references (`$subject.path`), assigned roles select which rules are in scope. `scopecontext` carries two things: type scopecontext = {
  subjects: record<string, record<string, unknown>>;
  assignedroles: string[];
}; ts reference › subjects example bag for the documents product: a rule fragment like `document.workspaceid:$session.workspace.id` resolves against `ctx.subjects.session.workspace.id`. a reference to a subject not present in the bag denies — see security (`security.md`). a subject is a top-level key in `subjects`. it groups related state — the session, the request, the tenant, anything the consumer wants rules to be able to reference. subjects are the contract between rule authors (who write `$subject.path`) and bag builders (who populate the data). const ctx: scopecontext = {
  subjects: {
    session: {
      user:      { id: "u-1", email: "alice@…" },
      workspace: { id: "w-1", type: "team" },
      team:      { id: "t-3" },
    },
  },
  assignedroles: ["workspace_member", "document_reader"],
}; ts reference › subjects declaration `assertvalidconfiguration({ ..., subjects })` validates every `$subject.path` in every rule against this list. undeclared subject references die at boot with the offending role pinpointed. a product with multiple subject groups (e.g. `session` + `request` + `tenant`) extends the list and adapts its bag-building to populate each. the consumer declares the set of allowed subject names at config time: // subjects.config.ts
export const subjects = ["session"] as const; ts reference › assigned roles where the consumer stores roles is a consumer choice — a column, a join table, a jwt claim, a cache. the library only sees the resolved array passed in. effective roles (assigned ∪ hierarchy-inherited) are computed inside the resolver, not during bag construction. see scope-filter (`scope-filter.md`). `assignedroles` is the caller's authorization claim — the list of scoped role names the consumer has determined apply to this request. the library reads from the array and does not look anywhere else for role information. reference › building the bag per request "trusted source" means the data is authoritative for that field — typically a database row keyed by an identity the auth provider has already verified. the library treats every value in the bag as truth; the consumer is responsible for not putting client-supplied data there. see security (`security.md`). the consumer constructs the bag from trusted sources before each call into the library: const ctx: scopecontext = {
  subjects: {
    session: {
      user:      await db.user.findbyid(verifiedsessionuserid),
      workspace: await db.workspace.findbyid(verifiedsessionworkspaceid),
      team:      await db.team.findbyid(verifiedsessionteamid),
    },
  },
  assignedroles: await rolesfor(verifiedsessionuserid, verifiedsessionworkspaceid),
}; ts reference › what the library never does  the library does not parse cookies, validate sessions, read databases, or talk to auth providers. the bag is the boundary: everything before it is the consumer's; everything after it is the library's. unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed

doc/gated-scoped-ac-dsl-rules:
dsl rules gated-scoped-ac  gated-scoped-ac consumer shared/libs/gated-scoped-ac   axioms any access-control business intention is expressible. rules adapt to any business model and any storage schema. roles are the unit of access; hierarchies compose them. rules can traverse entity relationships through joins declared in the schema. rules are declarations — meaning is independent of order or evaluation strategy. purpose **why.** a declarative language is auditable and changeable without touching code. every product needs the same shapes — roles, inheritance, conditional grants over related entities — and a shared dsl captures them once. **how.** roles bundle access. each rule attached to a role pairs a context (preconditions on the bag) with a target (the slice of an entity granted, possibly traversing relationships through joins). hierarchies declare inheritance: a role automatically grants everything its descendants grant. **why this way.** declarations decouple meaning from evaluation. the same rules behave identically regardless of when, where, or in what order they are consulted, which is what makes them auditable. roles + hierarchies + relationship traversal cover the access-control intentions products actually need without leaking product knowledge into the language. **what.** the language consumers use to declare access control: roles, hierarchies among roles, and per-operation rules that pair context preconditions with target scopes. reference  the examples in this document use a fictional document-management product: workspaces contain folders, folders contain documents, users belong to workspaces through memberships, optionally grouped into teams. none of this vocabulary is built into the library — it is what the consumer declares in their schema and rules. reference › role definition the library reads four fields: a role is a named bundle of access rules over one entity, for one or more operations. the library treats the role name as an opaque identifier; the role's meaning is whatever its rules grant. const roles = {
  workspace_owner: {
    entity: "document",
    actions: ["read", "create", "update", "delete"],
    accessrules: [
      // …rules here
    ],
    fields: undefined,                 // all columns reachable
  },
  document_reader: {
    entity: "document",
    actions: ["read"],
    accessrules: [
      // …rules here
    ],
    fields: ["id", "title", "preview"],
  },
}; ts ascii | field | required | meaning |
|-------|----------|---------|
| `entity` | yes | which entity the rules act on. must be declared in the schema. |
| `actions` | yes | which operations this role contributes to. a role with `["read"]` does not affect a `delete` decision. |
| `accessrules` | yes | list of rules. order has no semantic effect; rules combine by union. |
| `fields` | no | column allowlist. `undefined` = all columns. | reference › consumer-extended definitions the library reads only its four fields and ignores the rest. extending is the canonical way to keep one declaration object per role without contaminating the engine. the library type is intentionally minimal. consumers needing product-specific fields (ui labels, signals for external systems, anything outside engine evaluation) extend the type: type productroledefinition = scopedroledefinition & {
  title?: string;
  description?: string;
  // any other product-owned signal
}; ts reference › rules: context + target **context** — a list of scopes that must all match against the bag for the rule to apply. empty `context` always applies. **target** — a list of scopes that together describe the rows reachable by this rule. empty `target` grants the entity unrestricted (all rows). multiple rules on a role combine by union: any rule that matches contributes its target. a rule has two halves: {
  context: ["workspace.type:'team'"],          // preconditions on the bag
  target:  ["document.workspaceid:$session.workspace.id"],  // the slice granted
} ts reference › grammar the parser rejects unquoted literals and unprefixed dotted rhs values at boot. both context and target scopes use the same `lhs:rhs` shape. the class of each side is decided by its sigil: ascii | class | sigil | example | where it appears |
|-------|-------|---------|------------------|
| entity path | bare | `document.workspaceid` | lhs only — a path into an entity declared in the schema |
| subject path | `$` | `$session.user.id` | rhs only — a path into the runtime bag |
| literal | `'…'` | `'team'` | rhs only — a quoted string constant | rule         = contextscope | targetscope
contextscope = entitypath ':' literal              (no subject paths on context rhs)
targetscope  = entitypath ':' (subjectpath | literal)
entitypath   = identifier ('.' identifier)+
subjectpath  = '$' identifier ('.' identifier)+
literal      = "'" non-apostrophe-chars "'"
operator     = ':' | ':!'                          ('!' = not equal) reference › why context rhs is literal-only  context scopes are preconditions evaluated before any target work happens. allowing subject paths on context rhs produces resolution-time circular dependencies (the subject the context tests may itself depend on the result of the rule). restricting context rhs to literals removes the cycle and keeps preconditions pure. reference › operators  ascii | operator | meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `:` | equals |
| `:!` | not equals | workspace.type:'personal'        → workspace's type is "personal"
workspace.type:!'archived'       → workspace's type is anything except "archived"
document.ownerid:$session.user.id    → document's owner is the caller reference › hierarchy each key is a role name. its value is a (possibly empty) object of descendants. `{}` = leaf role (no further inheritance). the structure is purely declarative — order is irrelevant, depth is unbounded, the same role may appear under multiple parents. a user assigned `workspace_owner` effectively holds `workspace_owner` + `manage_workspace_members` + `manage_workspace_documents` + `view_workspace_documents`. the library expands assigned roles through the hierarchy at evaluation time — see scope-filter (`scope-filter.md`). roles compose through declared inheritance, expressed as a generic nested tree. a parent role automatically grants everything its descendants grant, plus its own rules. const hierarchy: rolehierarchyconfig = {
  workspace_owner: {
    manage_workspace_members: {},
    manage_workspace_documents: {
      view_workspace_documents: {},
    },
  },
  team_admin: {
    invite_team_members: {},
    view_team_documents: {},
  },
  manage_self: { view_self: {} },
}; ts reference › no special keys, no auto-injection the library also **never auto-adds roles** to a request. if a product wants every authenticated user to inherit a baseline set (e.g. "manage own profile"), the consumer is responsible for including those role names in the `assignedroles` array passed to `expandroles`. organizing the hierarchy by archetype, baseline-vs-org, or any other product taxonomy is a consumer concern — express it in how the consumer composes the tree from partials, not by relying on library-special keys. the hierarchy is a flat top-level map of roots. the library does not interpret any key as special (no `account_baseline`, no `orgtypes`, no archetype concept). every root is a sibling. // consumer middleware
const assignedroles = [
  ...productbaselinerolekeys,    // consumer's choice — library doesn't know
  ...member.scopedroles,         // from db
];
const effective = expandroles(assignedroles, hierarchy); ts reference › schema the schema records *what exists*, not *what ac does with it*. adding a role never requires schema changes. the dsl stays storage-agnostic by referencing entities through a schema map the consumer declares. the schema is the storage-binding for the engine — it tells the resolver which table backs each entity name, what its id column is, and which columns are reachable. const schema = {
  document: {
    table:   documentstable,    // storage handle (e.g. drizzle table object)
    id:      documentstable.id,
    columns: { id, workspaceid, folderid, ownerid, title },
  },
  folder: {
    table:   folderstable,
    id:      folderstable.id,
    columns: { id, workspaceid },
  },
  workspace: {
    table:   workspacestable,
    id:      workspacestable.id,
    columns: { id, ownerid },
  },
}; ts reference › traversal: the arrow reads as: "members whose `organizationid` joins to an `organization` whose `type` equals `'platform_admin'`." the resolver compiles this into a traversal filter: the engine assumes the fk joins to the toentity's `id` column. (this is the convention; explicit fk metadata is not declared in the schema.) multi-hop chains compose by adding arrows: each hop is one left join at sql emit time. hops sharing the same `(fromentity → toentity)` across multiple roles deduplicate to a single join. the role's `entity` is always the *target* — the thing the filter narrows. the chain starts on the target entity's column and walks outward. a target scope can chain across foreign keys with `→`. each arrow says "follow this column to the joined entity's id": member.organizationid → organization.type:'platform_admin' {
  type: "traversal",
  hops: [{ fromentity: "member", fkfield: "organizationid", toentity: "organization" }],
  finalcondition: { /* condition on organization */ },
} ts document.folderid → folder.workspaceid → workspace.ownerid:$session.user.id reference › direct vs. traversal at a glance  direct (no arrow)         org.id:$session.org.id
                          └── condition on the role's entity itself

traversal (one arrow)     member.organizationid → organization.type:'platform_admin'
                          └── follow fk, then condition on the joined entity

traversal (multi-hop)     document.folderid → folder.workspaceid → workspace.ownerid:$session.user.id
                          └── follow chain of fks, condition on the last reference › field-level access when multiple roles contribute to the same decision, fields combine permissively: `undefined` ∪ anything = `undefined` (the broadest wins) `["a", "b"]` ∪ `["b", "c"]` = `["a", "b", "c"]` (set union) the `fields` property on a role definition restricts which columns the resolver permits the caller to read. ascii | `fields` value | meaning |
|----------------|---------|
| `undefined` | all columns reachable |
| `["id", "title"]` | only these columns reachable | reference › validation at boot every entity path resolves to an entity declared in the schema. every subject reference (`$foo.bar`) names a subject declared in the consumer's `subjects` list. every literal is properly quoted and closed. every hierarchy edge points to a declared role; no cycles. a failure throws with the offending role and rule pinpointed. misconfigurations die at boot, not at runtime. `assertvalidconfiguration({ roles, hierarchy, schema, subjects })` runs at startup and validates: unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed

doc/gated-scoped-ac-internals:
internals gated-scoped-ac  gated-scoped-ac contributor shared/libs/gated-scoped-ac   axioms the engine exposes four crud methods and one expansion call: `expandroles`, `resolveread`, `resolveupdate`, `resolvecreate`, `resolvedelete`. reads and updates internally build a `scopefilter` and compile it to `resolvedscope` before returning. creates and deletes evaluate against `properties` and return `scopeverdict`. both compile and properties evaluation are internal. for each effective role whose context preconditions match the bag, the rule's target scope contributes to the decision. contributions combine permissively. `or` across rules and across roles for read/update; allow-if-any for create/delete; `fields` allowlists combine by union with `undefined` (broadest) shadowing. the `scopefilter` is a discriminated union; every variant is total, with no implicit defaults. the drizzle builder must handle every variant via `assertnever` exhaustiveness. purpose **why.** the engine's external surface returns `resolvedscope` and `scopeverdict`. contributors need to know what's underneath: the union of variants, the combination semantics, the compile rules. without this, changes to one layer break the other. **how.** the resolver evaluates each applicable rule into a `scopefilter` and combines them. for read/update, the combined filter is then handed to the drizzle builder, which switch-dispatches on the union variant and emits sql. for create/delete, no compile happens — target scopes are matched against `properties` directly. **why this way.** splitting evaluation from compile keeps the resolver storage-agnostic and unit-testable in isolation (a passthrough compile reduces resolver tests to pure-data assertions). the discriminated-union output is what makes the boundary contractual: every variant is named, every variant is total, every adapter must handle them all. **what.** the internal evaluation algorithm: how the resolver turns rules + bag into a `scopefilter`, how the drizzle builder compiles a `scopefilter` into `{ where, select, joins }`, and how the two compose into the engine's public methods. reference › public engine methods  expandroles(assignedroles, hierarchy): string[]

resolveread   (effective, entity, subjects):              resolvedscope
resolveupdate (effective, entity, subjects):              resolvedscope
resolvecreate (effective, entity, subjects, properties):  scopeverdict
resolvedelete (effective, entity, subjects, properties):  scopeverdict

explainscoperesolution(scopedrole, roles, ctx): scopeexplanation ts `expandroles` is a pure function over assigned roles + hierarchy (no bag, no compile). cacheable per session. the four crud methods take the bag explicitly and produce one decision each. reference › evaluation algorithm filter the role definitions to those whose `entity` matches and whose `actions` includes the requested operation. for each remaining role, for each rule: — evaluate every `context` scope against the bag. the role contributes only if all `context` scopes match. (empty `context` always matches.) — for each `target` scope, resolve the rhs: — — literal → literal value. — — `$subject.path` → walk the bag; if absent, the rule contributes nothing. — emit a `scopefilter` (read/update) or check the property match (create/delete). combine all per-rule contributions into one decision. order of evaluation is irrelevant — rules are declarative, the combinators are order-independent. for any `resolve*` call: reference › combination semantics  ascii | output | combination |
|---|---|
| filter (read/update) | `or` across rules, `or` across roles. and across target scopes within a single rule. |
| verdict (create/delete) | allow if any rule's context matches and all its target scopes match `properties`. deny otherwise. |
| `fields` allowlist | set union; `undefined` (full access) wins over any explicit list. | if no role contributes (none match, or none have the operation in `actions`), the filter is `{ type: "none" }` for reads/updates and `{ allow: false, reason }` for creates/deletes. reference › `scopefilter` shape (internal) **rows** — `type` and its payload describe which rows are reachable. **columns** — `fields` is the allowlist, present on every variant except `none`. the `traversal` variant is what the dsl `→` operator compiles into. each hop becomes one `left join` at sql emit; the `finalcondition` is itself a `scopefilter` applied to the last hop's entity. **outer rows survive the join** — a missing related row does not silently exclude the candidate from negation-style filters applied to the outer entity. type scopefilter =
  | { type: "all";         fields?: string[] }                                          // no row restriction
  | { type: "none";        fields?: never }                                             // no access
  | { type: "self";        id: string;                            fields?: string[] }   // single row by id
  | { type: "notself";     id: string;                            fields?: string[] }   // exclude single row
  | { type: "children";    parentid: string;                      fields?: string[] }   // rows by parent
  | { type: "notchildren"; parentid: string;                      fields?: string[] }   // exclude by parent
  | { type: "meta";        field: string; values: string[];
                           negate?: boolean;                      fields?: string[] }   // app-owned metadata json path
  | { type: "field";       entity: string; field: string;
                           value: string | string[];
                           negate?: boolean;                      fields?: string[] }   // generic field match
  | { type: "composite";   filters: scopefilter[];
                           logic: "and" | "or";                   fields?: string[] }   // combine filters (logic required)
  | { type: "traversal";
      hops: array<{ fromentity: string; fkfield: string; toentity: string }>;
      finalcondition: scopefilter;
                                                                  fields?: string[] }; // fk chain + condition on last entity ts two axes are encoded: reference › dsl → scopefilter mapping `entity.id:rhs` (single segment, field is "id") → `self` / `notself` `entity.parentid:rhs` (single segment, field is "parentid") → `children` / `notchildren` `entity.<metadatacol>.<key>:rhs` (multi-segment, first segment matches `schema[entity].metadata`) → `meta` otherwise (single-segment field path, no special meaning) → `field` with traversal arrows → `traversal` wrapping the inner filter `schema[entity].metadata` is what gates the meta variant — without that declaration, multi-segment field paths fail boot validation as `invalid_field_path`. the resolver maps parsed scopes to filter variants by inspection: reference › compile pipeline (drizzle adapter) `compile` switch-dispatches on `filter.type`: joins emitted by traversal are returned in `resolvedscope.joins` as `joindescriptor[]`. consumers apply via `for (const j of joins) q = q.leftjoin(j.table, j.on)`. `buildselect` returns the column map (full or restricted by the `fields` allowlist). `createscopefilterbuilder({ schema, sql })` returns: interface scopefilterbuilder {
  compile(entity: string, filter: scopefilter): resolvedscope;
  buildwhere(entity: string, filter: scopefilter): sql | undefined;
  buildselect(entity: string, filter: scopefilter): record<string, unknown>;
} ts ascii | variant | where | notes |
|---|---|---|
| `all` | `undefined` | no restriction |
| `none` | `1 = 0` | empty select |
| `self` / `notself` | `id = ?` / `id <> ?` | uses normalized id column |
| `children` / `notchildren` | `parentid = ?` / `parentid <> ?` | requires `parentid` column on entity |
| `field` | `<col> = ?` (or `in (...)` for array, with optional negation) | column lookup via schema |
| `meta` | `(metadata)::jsonb->>'<field>' = ?` | requires `metadata` column on the entity schema |
| `composite` | recursive and/or combination | `logic` is required, no fallback |
| `traversal` | `left join`s + recursive compile of `finalcondition` against last hop's entity | hops dedup by `(from.fk → to)` | reference › properties matching (create/delete) context scopes evaluated against bag (same as read/update). each target scope's lhs field path is walked into `properties`. if absent, the rule denies. rhs resolved (literal or `$subject.path` via bag), compared to the property value. operator (`eq` / `ne`) applied. missing values never satisfy any operator. allow if any rule's context matches and all its target scopes match `properties`. traversal targets on create/delete: only the `finalcondition`'s field path is walked into `properties` (hops are ignored — there's no joined data on a single-row decision). if the candidate row doesn't surface that field, the rule denies. the boot validator emits an `inert_traversal_target` warning for any role whose `actions` include `create` or `delete` and which uses a traversal target. for create/delete, no `scopefilter` is emitted. instead, the resolver walks each rule: reference › exhaustiveness contract an unhandled variant defaulting to "no restriction" is a critical security bug. the drizzle builder uses an exhaustive switch and a `customhandler` extension point for forward-compatibility with consumer-defined variants. every adapter that consumes `scopefilter` must handle every variant. use `assertnever(filter)` in the default branch: function buildwhere(filter: scopefilter): sql {
  switch (filter.type) {
    case "all":         return sql`true`;
    case "none":        return sql`false`;
    case "self":        return eq(table.id, filter.id);
    // … handle every variant
    default:            return assertnever(filter);   // compile-time error if any variant is missed
  }
} ts reference › field interpolation safety drizzle's parameterization handles values automatically — the residual risk is identifier injection (column / json-path names), which the library cannot prevent without knowing the consumer's schema policy. the `field` and `meta` variants carry consumer-routable strings (`field.field`, `meta.field`). the library does not sanitize them. adapters that interpolate these into raw sql (the meta variant uses raw `->>` against an identifier name) must validate against an allowlist: const allowed_meta_fields = ["type", "parentid", "status"];

case "meta":
  if (!allowed_meta_fields.includes(filter.field)) {
    throw new error(`unsanctioned meta field: ${filter.field}`);
  }
  // safe to interpolate ts unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed

doc/gated-scoped-ac-mount:
mount gated-scoped-ac  gated-scoped-ac consumer shared/libs/gated-scoped-ac   axioms the engine is constructed once at app boot. reconstructing per request is a misuse. the engine receives the drizzle schema and `sql` helpers directly. no intermediate normalization is the consumer's responsibility. the hono middleware receives the engine and the bag-builder. it does no auth itself. boot is the only place misconfiguration can be detected. the engine throws if rules, schema, or hierarchy are inconsistent. purpose **why.** authorization needs identical inputs across the app. constructing the engine once at boot and binding it to a middleware once gives every route the same engine, the same compile, and the same trust boundary. **how.** `createscopedac(...)` runs validation, builds the internal drizzle filter builder, and returns a `scopedac`. `createscopemiddleware({ engine, buildcontext })` wraps it for hono. the middleware calls `buildcontext(c)` per request, expands roles via the engine, and stashes a pre-bound `scopedacrequestcontext` on the hono context. **why this way.** the engine is a configured object, not a class hierarchy. the bag-builder is the trust boundary — keeping it as a function the consumer supplies makes it the explicit place where session/auth verification ends and authorization begins. **what.** the single mount point that turns a configured engine + a request bag-builder into a hono middleware. after mount, every route handler has `c.get("scopedac")` populated with effective roles and bound crud methods. reference › boot wiring  // app-level engine assembly — in your app's composition root (e.g. apps/api/src/wiring/)
import { createscopedac } from "@prowd/scoped-ac";
import { createscopemiddleware } from "@prowd/scoped-ac/adapters/hono";
import { eq, ne, and, or, not, inarray, sql } from "drizzle-orm";
import * as schema from "@/models";
import { roles, hierarchy, subjects } from "@configs/access-control";
import { createbuildscopecontext } from "@prowd/user-organization-mgmt/api";
import { db } from "@/lib/db";

const moduleentityschema = {
  organization: { table: schema.organization, metadata: schema.organization.metadata },
  member:       { table: schema.member },
  invitation:   { table: schema.invitation },
  // …one entry per entity referenced in any rule
};

export const engine = createscopedac({
  roles,
  hierarchy,
  subjects,
  schema: moduleentityschema,
  sql: { eq, ne, and, or, not, inarray, sql },
});

export const ac = {
  engine,
  middleware: createscopemiddleware({
    engine,
    buildcontext: createbuildscopecontext({ db }),
  }),
}; ts import { ac } from "./config/access-control.config";
app.use("*", ac.middleware); ts in your hono app setup: reference › inputs  ascii | field | source | trust |
|---|---|---|
| `roles` | `@configs/access-control` | config-time |
| `hierarchy` | `@configs/access-control` | config-time |
| `subjects` | `@configs/access-control` | config-time |
| `schema` | drizzle table objects from your models | config-time |
| `sql` | `drizzle-orm` exports | library |
| `buildcontext(c)` | returns `{ subjects, assignedroles } \| null` | **consumer-owned trust boundary** | `buildcontext` is called per request after auth has verified the session. return `null` to short-circuit with 401 (or 403 if `unauthenticatedstatus: 403` is configured). **never put unverified request body or unsigned cookie data into the bag** — see `security.md`. reference › what the engine returns  interface scopedac {
  expandroles(assignedroles: string[]): string[];

  resolveread   (effective, entity, subjects):              resolvedscope;   // { where, select, joins }
  resolveupdate (effective, entity, subjects):              resolvedscope;
  resolvecreate (effective, entity, subjects, properties):  scopeverdict;    // { allow, reason? }
  resolvedelete (effective, entity, subjects, properties):  scopeverdict;

  explainscoperesolution(role, ctx): scopeexplanation;

  readonly roles, hierarchy, schema, subjects;
} ts reference › what the request context holds in route handlers, use `getscopedaccontext(c)` instead of raw `c.get(...)` for type narrowing. `c.get("scopedac")` returns: interface scopedacrequestcontext {
  subjects;
  assignedroles;
  effectiveroles;
  resolveread   (entity):         resolvedscope;
  resolveupdate (entity):         resolvedscope;
  resolvecreate (entity, props):  scopeverdict;
  resolvedelete (entity, props):  scopeverdict;
  explainscoperesolution(role):   scopeexplanation;
} ts reference › boot validation entity referenced in a rule but missing from `schema` field referenced on an entity but missing from that entity's `columns` subject referenced as `$session.x` but not in `subjects` multi-segment field path that isn't the entity's declared metadata column metadata column declared but not present in `columns` hierarchy edge to an undeclared role (strict mode) rule with empty context and empty target ("god mode") (warning) rule with traversal target on a role that includes `create` or `delete` actions — that combination is inert these all surface at boot, not at first request. `createscopedac` runs `assertvalidconfiguration` internally. it throws `scopedacconfigerror` on: reference › optional config  ascii | field | default | effect |
|---|---|---|
| `strictgodmode` | `false` | when true, empty-context-and-empty-target rules fail boot |
| `contextkey` (middleware) | `"scopedac"` | hono context variable name |
| `unauthenticatedstatus` | `401` | status when `buildcontext` returns `null` | reference › where this engine and the middleware should not be reconstructed per request — the engine is stateless after boot; rebuilding is wasted work and bypasses validation inside a route handler — handlers consume `c.get("scopedac")`, not a fresh engine inside a repository — repos receive the engine via di from the module mount the only place that constructs the engine is the file you put your `createscopedac(...)` call in. unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed

