backend usage list-query  list-query consumer shared/libs/list-query  everything in this doc lives under `@prowd/list-query/server`. the root entry (`@prowd/list-query`) exposes only isomorphic types and the sort parser. `createlistqueryschema` **use `.extend()`, not `.merge()`.** zod's `.merge()` takes unknown-keys mode from the right-hand side (default: strip), silently losing the strict mode the factory applied. `.extend()` preserves it. this is the single most important gotcha of the factory. produces the validator every list endpoint composes with its own filters. import { z } from "zod";
import { createlistqueryschema } from "@prowd/list-query/server";
import { list_query_defaults } from "@configs/list-query";

export const getorganizationsqueryschema = createlistqueryschema({
  defaults: list_query_defaults,
  sortable: ["name", "createdat"] as const,
  defaultsort: "name",
  searchable: true,
}).extend({
  status: z.enum(["active", "inactive"]).optional(),
  type:   z.enum(["system_admin", "platform_admin", "organization"]).optional(),
}); ts `createlistqueryschema` › what you pass **`sortable`** — tuple of allowed sort field names. any token in the client's `sort` param that isn't in this list is a 400. **`defaultsort`** — mandatory default. used when the client sends no `sort`. same grammar as the `sort` param (`"name"`, `"-createdat"`, `"type,-name"`). the default itself must parse against `sortable`. **`searchable`** — if `true`, the schema accepts an optional `search` string. if `false` (or omitted), sending `search` is a 400. no silent ignoring. **`defaults`** — the three numeric defaults (`page`, `pagesize`, `maxpagesize`). typically `list_query_defaults` from `@configs/list-query`. omit for the lib baseline (1 / 25 / 100). `createlistqueryschema` › what you get strict means any unknown key is a 400. after `.extend({...})` the extended schema stays strict. a strict zodobject whose output type is: {
  page: number;
  pagesize: number;
  sort: sortspec[];      // always non-empty — default injected if absent
  search?: string;        // only present if searchable: true
} ts `createlistqueryschema` › validator rejections (all → 400)  ascii | input | reason |
|---|---|
| `page=0`, `page=-1`, `page=1.5`, `page=abc` | `page` must be positive int |
| `pagesize=101` (with default max) | exceeds `maxpagesize` |
| `sort=bogus` | unknown field (not in `sortable`) |
| `sort=name,-name` | duplicate field |
| `sort=name,` | empty token (trailing comma) |
| `search=...` on non-searchable endpoint | unknown key via strict |
| any other extra key | unknown key via strict | `buildorderby` the column map must cover every entry in the zod `sortable` whitelist for the same endpoint — the two stay in sync by convention. a runtime mismatch throws (it's a programmer bug, not a user bug). the tiebreaker is appended as `asc(...)` unless the caller already sorted by the same column identity, in which case the declared direction wins and no duplicate is added. null ordering follows postgresql defaults — we don't emit `nulls first/last`. translates `sortspec[]` into a drizzle `sql[]` with the tiebreaker auto-appended. import { buildorderby } from "@prowd/list-query/server";
import * as schema from "../models";

const orderby = buildorderby(
  query.sort,
  {
    name:      schema.organization.name,
    createdat: schema.organization.createdat,
  },
  schema.organization.id, // tiebreaker
); ts `applypagination`  import { applypagination } from "@prowd/list-query/server";

const listq = applypagination(
  base.orderby(...orderby),
  query.page,
  query.pagesize,
); ts equivalent to `.limit(pagesize).offset((page - 1) * pagesize)`. the helper exists so every list endpoint computes the offset identically and the intent stays visible at the call site. the "same where" rule the count query must use the exact same `where` expression as the list query. never diverge. the lib doesn't enforce this — it can't, the two queries are built separately. your code enforces it by building one `whereexpr` variable and passing it to both: one named variable, used twice. a diff that touches one `where` without the other is obvious during code review. that's the enforcement mechanism. this is the single most important backend invariant: const whereexpr = and(
  org.where,
  query.search ? ilike(schema.organization.name, `%${query.search}%`) : undefined,
  query.status ? sql`(${schema.organization.metadata})::jsonb->>'status' = ${query.status}` : undefined,
);

const base = this.db
  .select({ ...org.select } as selectedfields)
  .from(schema.organization)
  .where(whereexpr);                       // <-- same whereexpr

const listq  = applypagination(base.orderby(...orderby), query.page, query.pagesize);
const countq = this.db
  .select({ c: count() })
  .from(schema.organization)
  .where(whereexpr);                       // <-- same whereexpr

const [rows, [{ c }]] = await promise.all([listq, countq]);
return { rows, total: number(c) }; ts the "same where" rule › scoped count  when `engine.resolveforreadupdate(...)` + `scopefilterbuilder.compile(...)` is used, its `where` output must flow into `whereexpr` too. the count then respects the exact same scope filter as the list — unauthorised users see `total: 0` without ever hitting a 403. unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed

