doc/ui-cascade-spec:
cascade specification ui  ui contributor shared/libs/ui  technical details of how the theme cascade translates to css. for the conceptual model, see architecture (`architecture.md`). selector model  two attributes, never on the same node: <div element="surface--card">        element = {family}--{element}
<div layout="left-sidebar">          layout = bare name (no prefix) html selector model › part attribute rules: only emit css for `part` values **explicitly declared** in the theme under that surface's `parts` field. an undeclared `part` is inert — zero css emitted. this prevents developers from using `part` as a free styling escape hatch. `part` is never used on the same node as `element` or `layout`. selector emitted: `[element="surface--card"] [part="body"] { ... }` `part="name"` marks a structural sub-region of a themed ancestor. use it on plain divs that a designer needs to control (padding, gap, background) without giving the div a full element identity. <div element="surface--card">
  <div part="body">      ← designer controls interior here
  <div part="actions">   ← designer controls gap here html selector model › family prefixes (for element attribute)  ascii | family key              | prefix          |
|-------------------------|-----------------|
| surfacecontainer        | `surface`       |
| elevatedoverlay         | `overlay`       |
| interactivecontrol      | `control`       |
| interactivecontrolgroup | `control-group` |
| field                   | `field`         |
| listcontainer           | `list`          |
| shape                   | `shape`         |
| media                   | `media`         | layout uses `layout="value"` directly — no family prefix. selector model › axis attributes (bare, no data- prefix)  `emphasis`, `status`, `state`, `scale`, `concept` css specificity  css source order + specificity handles cascade priority. no variable chains or bridge systems. the engine emits rules in this order: [element^="surface--"]                                  l2 family (lowest)
[element="surface--card"]                               l3 element
[element="surface--card"][emphasis="primary"]            axis (emphasis first)
[element="surface--card"][status="error"]                axis (status later = wins)
[element="surface--card"]:hover                         pseudo-class (highest)
[element="surface--card"][emphasis="primary"]:hover      compound (most specific)
[concept="x"] [element="surface--card"]                 concept (2 selectors)
[element="a"] [element="b"]                             contextual (2 selectors) composition variables everything else is plain css. padding, radius, shadow, cursor, transition, typography — set directly, no variables. 5 css custom properties handle cross-property composition: ascii | variable | why it exists |
|----------|---------------|
| `--bg` | tint mixes into background via `color-mix()` |
| `--fg` | `smart-bw` computes foreground from background |
| `--tint` | one color modifies both background and border |
| `--border-color` | tint mixes into border via `color-mix()` |
| `--bg-alpha` | alpha composites with transparent | composition variables › property application (on [element] and [layout])  [element] {
  background: color-mix(in srgb,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--tint, var(--bg, transparent)) 20%, var(--bg, transparent))
    var(--bg-alpha, 100%),
    transparent
  );
  color: var(--fg, inherit);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb,
    var(--tint, var(--border-color, transparent)) 30%,
    var(--border-color, transparent)
  );
} css output principles **zero theme = zero cascade lines.** only structural plumbing remains. **only emit what the theme defines.** no defaults copied to elements. **deltas only.** elements don't repeat family values. **one file, all modes.** default on `:root`, others on `[data-mode]`. **readable in devtools.** each rule shows what it does. smart bleed css inner radius = outer radius - border-width (child sits inside parent border). for `border` mode, child extends over the border, so outer radius is used. for `rounded` mode, interior edges also get radius. resolved at build time. the engine computes the parent's padding, radius, and border-width from the cascade, then emits: /* horizontal bleed into parent padding */
[selector] {
  margin-left: calc(-1 * <parent-px>);
  margin-right: calc(-1 * <parent-px>);
  padding-left: <parent-px>;
  padding-right: <parent-px>;
}

/* first child: bleed top + inherit parent top corners */
[selector]:first-child {
  margin-top: calc(-1 * <parent-py>);
  padding-top: <parent-py>;
  border-top-left-radius: calc(<parent-radius-tl> - <parent-border-width>);
  border-top-right-radius: calc(<parent-radius-tr> - <parent-border-width>);
  border-bottom-left-radius: 0;   /* straight interior edge (normal mode) */
  border-bottom-right-radius: 0;
}

/* last child: bleed bottom + inherit parent bottom corners */
[selector]:last-child { ... } css unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed

doc/ui-theme-authoring:
theme authoring ui  ui consumer shared/libs/ui  how to write a theme object. a theme is a typescript object conforming to the `theme` type (`shared/libs/ui/types/theme.ts`). theme object structure  theme
├── id, name
│
├── colors                         l1 primitive
│   ├── main (white, black)
│   ├── brand (1–4)
│   ├── status (success, info, warning, error → can reference flat)
│   ├── flat (11 accent colors)
│   └── semantictokens (primary, secondary, accent, muted)
│
├── typography                     l1 primitive
│   ├── fonts (primary, secondary, quote, monoprimary, monosecondary)
│   └── types (18 scales, each: fontfamily, fontstyle, fontsizemobile,
│              fontsizedesktop, fontweight, lineheight, letterspacing)
│
├── spacing                        l1 primitive (unit multiplier → xs..3xl)
├── radius                         l1 primitive (none, xs, sm, md, lg, xl, full)
├── effects                        l1 primitive (named effect presets)
├── transitions                    l1 primitive (duration + timing scales)
├── borderwidths                   l1 primitive (none → extrathick)
├── icons                          l1 primitive (set, sizes, weight)
│
├── modes                          cross-cutting
│   └── colorscheme
│       └── each mode: background + foreground anchors + derivation params
│
├── families                       l2 — morphological family defaults
│   ├── surfacecontainer           (is the base shape)
│   ├── elevatedoverlay            (surface + overlay extras)
│   ├── listcontainer              (surface + row/cell/separator/alternating)
│   ├── interactivecontrol         (surface wrapper)
│   ├── interactivecontrolgroup    (surface wrapper)
│   ├── field                      (surface + placeholder/caret/obfuscation)
│   ├── shape                      (surface wrapper)
│   └── media                      (surface + objectfit/aspectratio/overlay/filter)
│
├── elements                       l3 — element overrides (sparse deltas)
│
├── concepts                       l4 — product-specific semantic overrides
│   └── [conceptname]: { [target]: surfacecontainertokens + accent + contextual }
│
└── contextual                     l5 — parent → child overrides
    └── [parent]: { [child]: surfacecontainertokens }
        keys are element names (e.g. 'sidebar', 'header', 'card')
        matching the keys in elementtypes. not the full attribute
        value (not 'surface--sidebar'). color references  theme token values can reference colors by name: ascii | type | examples |
|------|----------|
| primitive | `'main-white'`, `'brand-1'`, `'status-error'`, `'flat-blue'` |
| derived | `'gray-200'`, `'brand-1-light'`, `'status-error-dark'` |
| semantic | `'primary'`, `'secondary'`, `'accent'`, `'muted'` |
| mode anchors | `'background'`, `'foreground'` (resolved from active color mode, not semantic tokens) |
| special | `'tint'` (current tint value), `'smart-bw'` (auto black/white) |
| css | `'transparent'`, `'currentcolor'`, `'inherit'` |
| raw hex | `'#ff0000'` (escape hatch, avoid when possible) | color references › which colour to write a surface's fill is `'background'`; its text is `'foreground'`. the two swap in dark mode. text on a coloured fill (a brand, a status) is `'smart-bw'`. `'main-white'` and `'main-black'` are written only where the colour must not swap: the mode anchors and the shadows. a tinted ground is a brand-tinted gray (`'brand-1-gray-50'`), with the amount in the mode's `graycolormixpercentage`. modes  each color mode defines background/foreground anchors and derivation parameters. the engine computes gray scales, brand light/dark, status light/dark from these. modes: {
  colorscheme: {
    light: {
      background: 'main-white',
      foreground: 'main-black',
      derivation: {
        brandlightamount: 74,
        branddarkamount: 20,
        statuslightamount: 74,
        statusdarkamount: 20,
        graycolormixpercentage: '5%',
        flatsaturationadjust: 0,
      },
    },
    dark: { ... },
  },
} typescript concepts concepts apply to both elements and layouts. `concept="dashboard"` on a shell → the theme overrides `maincontent` background for dashboard pages. group overrides by business meaning, not by element type: concepts: {
  knowledge: {
    card: {
      accent: 'brand-2',
      boundary: { border: { width: 'default', color: 'gray-pure-500' } },
      contextual: {
        header: {
          smartbleed: 'normal',
          interior: { background: 'brand-2', foreground: 'smart-bw' },
        },
      },
    },
  },
  dashboard: {
    maincontent: {
      interior: { background: 'gray-50' },
    },
  },
} typescript size variants default scale when absent: `md`. elements that support sizes define them at l3: button: {
  surface: { /* base */ },
  sizes: {
    xs: { partialsurface: { ... }, icon: { size: 'xs' } },
    sm: { partialsurface: { ... }, icon: { size: 'sm' } },
    // ...
  },
} typescript tint pattern  define emphasis as a visual pattern, use tint for the color: interactivecontrol: {
  surface: {
    emphasis: {
      primary: {
        partialsurface: {
          tint: 'primary',                      // accent color
          interior: { background: 'tint', foreground: 'smart-bw' },
          boundary: { border: { color: 'transparent' } },
        },
      },
    },
    status: {
      error: { partialsurface: { tint: 'status-error' } },
      // status only overrides tint → pattern stays, color changes
    },
  },
} typescript unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed

doc/value-types-readme:
@libs/value-types value-types  value-types shared/libs/value-types  universe of valid values for shared vocabulary backed by iso/iana/bcp 47/e.164 standards. pure data + thin validators. **zero product knowledge.** what it solves  across most products you end up with the same five value types — locale, timezone, phone, country, currency — duplicated across i18n json, zod schemas, and `<selectitem>` lists. this lib is the upstream universe each product narrows in its own preferences config. layering  shared/libs/value-types/        ← this lib: universe of valid values
        ↓
configs/<product>/              ← product narrows + sets defaults
        ↓
shared/modules/<module>/        ← per-user overrides + resolver the lib never imports from `configs/`, `modules/`, or `apps/`. subpaths  import { phoneschema } from "@libs/value-types/phone";
import { localeschema, locales } from "@libs/value-types/locale";
import { istimezone, timezones, grouptimezonesbyregion } from "@libs/value-types/timezone";
import { countryschema, getcountrymeta } from "@libs/value-types/country";
import { currencyschema, getcurrencymeta } from "@libs/value-types/currency"; ts import { phoneschema, localeschema, currencyschema } from "@libs/value-types"; ts or all at once: what each subfolder exports  ascii | subfolder | tuple | type | schema | metadata helper |
|-----------|-------|------|--------|-----------------|
| `locale`   | `locales`     | `locale`     | `localeschema`     | `getlocalemeta(code)` (cldr via `intl.displaynames`) |
| `timezone` | `timezones`*  | `timezone`   | `timezoneschema`** | `grouptimezonesbyregion()` |
| `phone`    | —             | `phone`      | `phoneschema`      | `ise164(value)`, `e164_regex` |
| `country`  | `countries`   | `country`    | `countryschema`    | `getcountrymeta(code)` (name, dialing code, flag) |
| `currency` | `currencies`  | `currency`   | `currencyschema`   | `getcurrencymeta(code)` (name, symbol, decimals) | \* `timezones` is read from `intl.supportedvaluesof("timezone")` at module load. \** `timezoneschema` is a runtime `refine` backed by `intl.datetimeformat`, not `z.enum`. adding a new value to the curated set for `timezone`: nothing — the runtime list reflects what the js engine knows. for `phone`: there's no curated list; e.164 covers the universe by construction. for `country` / `currency` / `locale`: append to the data file. no other change needed — types and the zod schema widen automatically. unreviewed unreviewed unreviewed
